WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:05.230
With Alien: Covenant out on Blu-ray, a stack
of new footage is now available including

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:08.910
a bonus scene that reveals David's plans for
an Alien queen.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:12.830
Yippee-ki-yay movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and in this video I'll be explaining a brand

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:17.369
new epilogue scene with new connections to
the original first and second Alien films,

00:00:17.369 --> 00:00:20.939
plus what we'll likely be seeing in the Alien:
Covenant sequel.

00:00:20.939 --> 00:00:25.609
I'll also run through many of the official
Blu-ray deleted and extended scenes and explain

00:00:25.609 --> 00:00:28.640
some of the gaps from the theatrical release
that they help fill in.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.600
Also, I'm starting a brand new Funko Pop giveaway
for an Alien: Covenant Funko Pop from this

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:34.600
range.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:38.080
To enter the giveaway, subscribe and leave
me a comment with your thoughts on the the

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:40.450
movie's bonus or deleted scenes.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:44.250
Just before I start: a quick warning that
I will be discussing spoilers, so take care

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.560
if you haven't seen the movie yet.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.160
Right, let's kick off with the bonus scene
"Advent", a 6-and-a-half-minute short film

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:53.710
which serves as a kind of epilogue to the
movie.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:58.530
It begins with David transmitting a secure
message to the Weyland-Yutani corporation.

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:02.010
David says he's extending an olive branch
to the company, which immediately opens up

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:06.061
all sorts of questions about what exactly
has happened between David and the corporation

00:01:06.061 --> 00:01:10.990
between the end of the movie and now for him
to be trying to reconcile with them, for example,

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:14.990
have they discovered his lies about the crew
dying in the neutrino blast or did they find

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:18.060
out he was impersonating Walter at the close
of the film?

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:22.530
As David talks, we get to see lots of details
of his workshop and the experiments he's been

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.780
conducting on the Paradise planet.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.619
In the message, David explains he's been exploring
space, following Peter Weyland's vision, and

00:01:29.619 --> 00:01:33.520
that together with Elizabeth Shaw he found
the Engineer planet which he describes as

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:35.399
a "rotting paradise".

00:01:35.399 --> 00:01:39.380
David says he wiped the planet clean as "a
gift to [Shaw]" and that he wanted to build

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:43.119
a new civilisation with her help, however
"she refused".

00:01:43.119 --> 00:01:47.180
It seems that after bombing the planet with
the deadly pathogen, David woke Shaw up and

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:50.369
she was presumably horrified at his actions.

00:01:50.369 --> 00:01:54.819
David has this totally creepy way of very
matter-a-factly talking about something that's

00:01:54.819 --> 00:01:59.920
actually truly horrifying, and explains that
he had no option but to use Shaw's body parts

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:01.670
to make his "masterpiece".

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:05.220
Of course, David's actions and the way he
talks about them are disturbing enough as

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:09.810
it is, but if you think back to the prologue
short "The Crossing" which shows David talking

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:14.740
about how Elizabeth Shaw showed him such kindness
and basically put him back together after

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:20.379
the events of Prometheus, what David does
and says in Advent becomes even more chilling.

00:02:20.379 --> 00:02:24.290
David believes the Engineers were too afraid
of their own power, which is clearly not a

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:28.930
problem he shares, and although both he and
they were creators of what he calls "the wolf

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:33.410
and the lamb", the Engineers were too scared
of their aggressive creation.

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:37.269
David then explains that as he researched
the black goo, he discovered it was "ripe

00:02:37.269 --> 00:02:42.550
with advanced nanoparticles" and functions
according to an "algorithm based on evolutionary

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:43.550
computing".

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:48.599
He also describes the living organism as a
form of radical and chaotic artificial intelligence,

00:02:48.599 --> 00:02:52.400
which is interesting because it ties in with
Ridley Scott's belief, which he's mentioned

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:57.011
in interviews and his director's commentary,
that in the real world, both A.I. and alien

00:02:57.011 --> 00:03:00.250
life could be potential threats to humanity.

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:04.510
David elaborates on his discoveries, revealing
that the pathogen adapts to each species of

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:07.030
living organism uniquely and alters it.

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:11.340
As he explains more details about what he's
recorded, the video flashes through various

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:15.340
images, with one image in particular revealing
that the fourth generation has a shortened

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:20.260
gestation period and additionally that his
creations have a hive-like mind that connect

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:21.620
them to a common goal.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:25.861
Now, this mention of a hive with a common
goal is a clear connection to the idea of

00:03:25.861 --> 00:03:30.879
an Alien queen which first appeared in James
Cameron's Aliens movie, the sequel to Ridley

00:03:30.879 --> 00:03:32.580
Scott's original Alien.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:36.519
David explains that he didn't find the animal
life on the Engineer's planet suitable enough

00:03:36.519 --> 00:03:41.819
to fully develop his alien creation, but that
he discovered Shaw's human body was a much

00:03:41.819 --> 00:03:45.939
better host, which he used to finally develop
what he calls his "wolf".

00:03:45.939 --> 00:03:50.070
The video then jumps back to the Covenant
ship to remind us, as David says, that he

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:55.170
now also has his "flock of lambs", in other
words, the ship's human cargo, who he views

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:59.120
as fodder for the continued creation of his
masterpiece and his experiments.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.470
And next we get the biggest tease for what's
coming up in the next sequel when David says

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:08.590
that he's still working on perfecting his
queen, then his hand touches Daniels' cryo

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:10.019
chamber!

00:04:10.019 --> 00:04:14.860
As this first message ends, David triumphantly
states that "this is going to change everything".

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:19.790
So, we've got a big tease here from Ridley
Scott for the follow-up movie to Alien: Covenant

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:24.400
that either Daniels will be the source of
David's alien queen or possibly even that

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:29.160
she'll be transformed into that, and it will
be interesting to see whether Scott will link

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:33.470
her in some way to James Cameron's alien queen
from Aliens, especially given there were some

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:37.930
visual similarities between the queen from
Aliens and the elongated head of the Xenomorph

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:42.130
we saw David develop in Alien: Covenant and
also given the fact that, in David's notes

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:46.020
which we see in the Advent sequence, he mentions
a hive mentality.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:49.830
More importantly though, I imagine, for Scott
will be linking up all the prequels to his

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:54.610
own Alien movie which kicked off the franchise
in 1979, so I'm guessing we'll be getting

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:58.740
an explanation for the source of all the eggs
on the Derelict where they first appeared

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:00.460
in that original movie.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:04.639
I'm just theorising, but I wonder whether
David discovered that female human eggs are

00:05:04.639 --> 00:05:09.150
the perfect host for developing his alien
creations because in Advent, he explains that

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:13.500
"there was something extraordinary about the
substance's reaction to the human genome"

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:18.060
and that he "was able to unlock new properties
and tweak the organism's aggression and instinct

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:19.060
for survival."

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:20.060
The Advent video then continues with four
more separate transmissions from David to

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:21.060
Weyland-Yutani which include some intriguing
visuals of David's research.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:23.610
Also interesting is that the start of each
of these four transmissions is labelled with

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:28.660
a Special Order code, all of which precede
Special Order 937 which was the directive

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:32.389
from Weyland-Yutani to Ash we saw in the first
Alien movie.

00:05:32.389 --> 00:05:37.880
Special Order 937 stated that the Nostromo's
primary objective was to collect and preserve

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:42.310
the alien creature discovered in that film
and the ship's crew was considered expendable

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:43.550
if necessary.

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:47.550
The special orders seen in Advent suggest
that David was in regular communication with

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:51.910
Weyland-Yutani about his research and that
the corporation's discussions with him led

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:57.020
them to consider the alien species an extremely
valuable potential bioweapon by the time Ripley

00:05:57.020 --> 00:06:00.069
and co arrived in the original Alien movie.

00:06:00.069 --> 00:06:03.990
Just how much the corporation is in league
with David in terms of making suggestions

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:08.509
about his warped experiments is open to question,
but we do get one final transmission from

00:06:08.509 --> 00:06:13.009
him in the Advent scene in which he declares
ominously, "If you do this, there will be

00:06:13.009 --> 00:06:14.360
no turning back.

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:17.270
My creatures shall be set loose to rule this
galaxy."

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:21.580
So, that was the Advent bonus scene which
provides a stack of new connections to the

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:22.580
original movies.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:26.690
The scene wouldn't have worked particularly
well as a final scene because it lacks the

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:32.169
drama of David putting Daniels into cryo sleep
just as she realises who he is and the reveal

00:06:32.169 --> 00:06:35.349
that David has brought a pair of alien embryos
on board.

00:06:35.349 --> 00:06:40.170
However, a cut-down version of the Advent
scene could have been a really good post-credits

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:41.170
tag.

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:44.160
Just like the prologue scenes which weren't
included in the movie, but would have made

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:48.039
it much better, this scene would have also
filled in some more gaps about what David

00:06:48.039 --> 00:06:49.419
really did with Shaw.

00:06:49.419 --> 00:06:52.949
By the way I made a full video about the missing
prologue scenes which you can check out by

00:06:52.949 --> 00:06:55.889
tapping the top-right of the screen.

00:06:55.889 --> 00:07:00.050
Michael Fassbender's performance as David
and Walter was, for me, the highlight of Alien:

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:04.169
Covenant and there's a longer opening scene
to the movie featuring the android villain

00:07:04.169 --> 00:07:06.139
which wasn't used in the final film.

00:07:06.139 --> 00:07:10.610
The opening prologue scene in the movie depicts
David's interactions with his creator Peter

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:13.020
Weyland just after he's been activated.

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:18.130
The extended version adds a little more dialogue
with Weyland asking David to explain the story

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:22.780
behind Wagner's "The Entry of the Gods into
Valhalla", the music David chose to play on

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:24.849
the piano for his creator.

00:07:24.849 --> 00:07:29.610
David tells the music's story about the gods
rejecting "mankind as weak, cruel and filled

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:34.570
with greed", and adds that the gods themselves
are false and doomed to "die in a cataclysmic

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:39.039
fire, [since they were] as venal as the humans
they rejected and their power is an illusion."

00:07:39.039 --> 00:07:43.430
This additional commentary feels a bit too
on the nose about David's opinion of people

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:48.360
like Weyland and it's also a little too obvious
a nod to the engineers and how David deals

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:50.000
with them later in the film.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:54.539
Likewise, it seems overly clear a tease of
how David himself will likely also become

00:07:54.539 --> 00:07:59.320
a victim of his own hubris in a future sequel
as he becomes blinded by his belief that he's

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:04.110
the one who's the perfect creator, not Weyland
or the engineers.

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:08.110
Another change to the opening of the movie
is a deleted scene of Walter in a Greenhouse

00:08:08.110 --> 00:08:09.199
on the Covenant.

00:08:09.199 --> 00:08:13.349
In the scene, Walter is busy checking on plants
in the greenhouse and has a humorous debate

00:08:13.349 --> 00:08:16.789
with Mother about whether he's whistling to
the plants to help them grow.

00:08:16.789 --> 00:08:20.860
Interestingly, the song we hear him whistle
is Twinkle, Twinkle Little Star, which is

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:25.270
an easter egg as both Alien: Resurrection
and Prometheus featured that track in its

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:27.599
trailers or TV spots.

00:08:27.599 --> 00:08:31.520
Walter and Mother's conversation is cut short
when the ship is hit by the neutrino blast

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:32.570
that damages it.

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:36.610
In the theatrical release, the neutrino blast
happened when Walter was checking on the Covenant's

00:08:36.610 --> 00:08:41.100
human cargo, in particular the ship's human
embryos and that probably worked better than

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:45.360
including this scene as that moment links
nicely to the scene at the end of the movie

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:49.100
when David smuggles in the pair of alien embryos.

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:54.250
James Franco's Branson was unfortunately hardly
in the theatrical release of Alien: Covenant,

00:08:54.250 --> 00:08:59.040
with his role whittled down to a brief appearance
in his cryo chamber and then in a small video

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:00.690
message cameo.

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:05.329
Franco did have some speaking lines in a prologue
scene that wasn't included in the final movie,

00:09:05.329 --> 00:09:09.760
but there was also a full deleted scene with
Branson that explored in more detail his relationship

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:11.040
with Daniels.

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:15.030
It's a flashback scene where Daniels dreams
she's back in her New York apartment together

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:16.030
with him.

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:19.700
In the scene, Branson discusses the design
of the cabin the couple are planning to build

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:20.700
on Origae-6.

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:24.700
In a way, it's a shame this scene didn't make
it in to the final film as it would have helped

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:29.380
the audience establish a stronger connection
to the couple and it also reinforces exactly

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:32.320
why the cabin was so important to Daniels.

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:36.959
There's an intriguing extended clip of the
Covenant team following David across the citadel

00:09:36.959 --> 00:09:40.690
courtyard which is filled with the dead bodies
of the engineers.

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:44.970
As they cross the plaza, the additional scene
shows the team stopping on a long bridge over

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:47.020
the side of which they throw a flare.

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:51.180
As the flare lights up what's below, they
see several enormous juggernauts, confirming

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:55.880
that the Engineers clearly had multiple ships
they used for travelling to other planets.

00:09:55.880 --> 00:09:59.649
It's possible that the scene ended up being
deleted because it might have led the Covenant

00:09:59.649 --> 00:10:04.450
crew to be suspicious about David's trustworthiness
too early on in the movie given his claim

00:10:04.450 --> 00:10:08.570
that he was marooned when his ship crashed,
and it also might have created confusion as

00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:10.950
to why David hadn't left the planet.

00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:15.589
There are also various other extended and
deleted scenes which wouldn't have added an

00:10:15.589 --> 00:10:19.930
awful lot to the final film and it's easy
to see why they were cut, although there are

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:23.950
three which do show a bit more of the bond
that Walter develops with Daniels and hint

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:27.320
more strongly at the emotional connection
he felt towards her.

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:30.870
Had they been included in the movie, these
scenes, which are the extended "Jacob's Funeral",

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:35.220
and the deleted "Walter Visits Daniels" and
"Daniels Thanks Walter", would have shown

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:39.950
the audience more clearly the affection between
Walter and Daniels, and would have provided

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:44.150
more obvious context for the moment in the
final film when David talks about Walter's

00:10:44.150 --> 00:10:46.899
love for Daniels and his own for Shaw.

00:10:46.899 --> 00:10:50.380
Now, what do you think about David's tease
of an Alien queen?

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:54.180
Does it make you more or less excited for
the next Alien prequel movie?

